<h2>What Is an Ontology — Not a Warehouse of Data but a Map of Meaning</h2>

<p>When companies evaluate an ontology project, the questions practitioners ask are strikingly similar. "So what exactly is an ontology?", "Is it the same as a graph DB?", "LLMs keep getting better — why would we need this?" A webinar conversation between the CEO of a Korean ontology-solutions company and a partner at a global consulting firm collected the questions they hear most often in the field, and this article uses those concept questions as its skeleton, reinforced with standards and research material. The practical questions — adoption, building, ROI — are covered in <a href="#!gdb-onto-adoption">the next part</a>.</p>

<h3>A database is a warehouse; an ontology is a map</h3>
<p>Start with the most basic distinction. A database is <strong>a place that physically stores data</strong>. An ontology is <strong>a structure that defines meaning and relationships</strong> on top of it. The conversation's analogy is exact: as a map tells you where you are and what connects to what, an ontology tells you what the data means and how it links together.</p>

