<p>The traffic analogy sharpens it further. The road a car drives on is infrastructure, that is, data. But roads alone do not make a traffic system — you also need the <strong>rules and meanings</strong>: this road is one-way, that one only admits certain vehicles, that stretch is hazardous. That layer of rules is the ontology. Academically, Tom Gruber's 1993 definition — <strong>"an explicit specification of a conceptualization"</strong> — is still the standard, and the W3C formalized it for exchange on the web as <strong>OWL 2</strong>, the Web Ontology Language.</p>

<p>The conversation names four essential components of an ontology — the domain's <strong>core concepts</strong>, the <strong>relationships</strong> between them, the actual <strong>data</strong>, and the <strong>rules</strong> the business runs on. Only with all four does a bare value like "inventory went up" become knowledge you can interrogate: was it a supplier's lead-time change, or a planner's strategic call?</p>

<h3>The semantic layer — an interpreter between legacy and generative AI</h3>
<p>So where does this map live in the enterprise stack? The answer: <strong>in the middle, between existing systems and generative AI</strong>. That middle tier is commonly called the semantic layer, and the ontology is its core component.</p>

