<p>Here comes the part practitioners are most relieved to hear: <strong>this is not a migration that rips out your ERP and CRM.</strong> Legacy stays put; you define in this layer what the legacy data means and how it relates, and the LLM consults the layer when it searches or reasons. The pattern is already proven in industry — most prominently, Palantir calls its ontology <strong>"the digital twin of an organization"</strong>, the same structure of a semantic layer of objects, links, and actions laid over data assets.</p>

<h3>Won't better LLMs make ontologies unnecessary?</h3>
<p>The sharpest question of the set. If an LLM could understand legacy information as-is and always answer correctly, you would not need an ontology. The problem is that <strong>hallucination</strong> is more serious than expected — and in mission-critical work where a single number must not be wrong (semiconductor yield calculations, insurance claim adjudication), a "mostly right" answer is useless.</p>
<p>The principle by which an ontology reduces hallucination is simple: instead of leaving everything to the LLM, <strong>constrain it to answer only within what the structure defines</strong>. The company in the conversation described inserting its own ontology query language between the question and the LLM, so the LLM never gets the degrees of freedom — like composing query strings — where hallucination creeps in (the language was patented and then opened for anyone to use, they said). The principle is the same lineage as the "fish with vectors, walk with graphs" approach in this wiki's <a href="#!gdb-graphrag">GraphRAG article</a> — attaching a deterministic structure to a probabilistic model.</p>
<p>External research reports the same direction. OG-RAG (EMNLP 2025), which retrieves over an ontology-grounded hypergraph, reported <strong>+55% fact recall and +40% response correctness</strong> across four LLMs, and a clinical question-answering study using ontology-grounded knowledge graphs cut <strong>hallucination from 63% to 1.7%</strong>. The company in the conversation shared its own measurements showing large hallucination drops when its ontology is combined with a base LLM (see the fact-check note below). The numbers vary by setting, but the direction is consistent — <strong>with structure in place, the model has less room to make things up.</strong></p>
<p>And when LLMs improve further? The conversation's outlook is measured: reasoning will keep getting better, but <strong>a company's internal data stays outside what an LLM can reach through pretraining</strong>. So the direction of progress is not "ontologies become unnecessary" but "LLMs get better at using structures like ontologies".</p>

<h3>Is it the same as a graph database?</h3>
<p>The misunderstanding that almost always follows. The answer is no. A graph DB is <strong>one strong storage technology</strong> for implementing an ontology, and inside a real ontology system three kinds of database split the work.</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Storage</th><th>What it is good at</th><th>Question it serves</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>RDB</strong></td><td>Storing and aggregating structured data</td><td>"How many units in stock this month?"</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Vector DB</strong></td><td>Similarity search over unstructured documents</td><td>"Find past incidents similar to this failure"</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Graph DB</strong></td><td>Traversing relationships and paths between entities</td><td>"If we close this valve, which reactors are affected?"</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Some questions are answered straight from the RDB, some fish similar cases out of vectors, and questions that must follow causality and impact ride the graph. An ontology's real skill shows in <strong>the architecture that combines the three to fit the question</strong> — in the conversation's words, many solutions claim the ontology label, but go one level down and look at how they combine the three stores to answer, and you see the actual capability. The two competing graph storage models themselves (RDF vs. property graph) are covered in <a href="#!news-20260711-neo4j-rdf-vs-property-graph">a separate article</a>.</p>

